                ],
                "translations": [
                    {
                        "translation_id": "48e13da2-94b1-4931-8b11-5b0c15290ce4",
                        "source_entry_id": "35719bcb-e8b7-3461-be3e-9a03d7af6f22",
                        "target_lang_word": "Taylorian",
                        "target_lang_code": "en",
                        "target_sense_ids": [],
                        "cross_references": [
                            {
                                "target_word": "Taylorian",
                                "target_lang_code": "en",
                                "target_senses": [
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "a16ba198-32ee-37fb-846a-1f18059d3f95",
                                        "entry_id": "6cde71e6-febd-38fd-beb8-7cdee3d3b320",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or relating to the English architect Sir Robert Taylor (1714–1788) or the Taylor Institution he founded at Oxford University for the study of European languages."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "e6b64227-2669-3c3f-bb08-21a33bac4760",
                                        "entry_id": "6cde71e6-febd-38fd-beb8-7cdee3d3b320",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or relating to Taylorism."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": []
                                    },
                                    {
                                        "sense_id": "ad7f19da-8f08-323f-99c8-669da64b102f",
                                        "entry_id": "6cde71e6-febd-38fd-beb8-7cdee3d3b320",
                                        "glosses": [
                                            "Of or relating to the Canadian philosopher Charles Taylor (born 1931)."
                                        ],
                                        "tags": [],
                                        "examples": [
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "3ed976cd-5311-406e-8ce8-b5870dee8775",
                                                "sense_id": "ad7f19da-8f08-323f-99c8-669da64b102f",
                                                "text": "The problem is basically that any mechanical process of cause and effect may be reinterpreted as a \"goal-directed\" process along Taylorian lines by simply supposing that whatever happens to be its outcome has all along been the \"end\" toward which the events comprising the process have been \"directed\"."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "547eaef9-7332-4c8b-a050-0262cedc829a",
                                                "sense_id": "ad7f19da-8f08-323f-99c8-669da64b102f",
                                                "text": "Later, constructing a notable Taylorian matrix, he argues that Eastonian economic-model theories of political life ‘always end up either laughable, or begging the major question, or both’ (p. 76)."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "899060a5-e182-4995-9e93-009f14e0067f",
                                                "sense_id": "ad7f19da-8f08-323f-99c8-669da64b102f",
                                                "text": "The formation of neighbourhoods within a particular school population may be seen as a counterpart to a Taylorian notion of \"distinctness\" and survivance."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "4998c8ba-dfc7-4a0f-b93c-0c00c71c7a5e",
                                                "sense_id": "ad7f19da-8f08-323f-99c8-669da64b102f",
                                                "text": "The U.N. report, while not a philosophical document, nevertheless points to facets of identity-formation that indicate a need to modify the Taylorian account."
                                            },
                                            {
                                                "example_id": "c5efdb7a-f099-4f4b-b2f4-cef4df4edfc7",
                                                "sense_id": "ad7f19da-8f08-323f-99c8-669da64b102f",
                                                "text": "The first one hundred pages of the book struck me as very Taylorian. Ratzinger’s description of the believer and the unbeliever standing side-by-side whispering “perhaps” into each other’s ears sounds not unlike Charles Taylor’s suggestion that we can’t help but live our faith “in a condition of doubt and uncertainty.”"
                                            }
                                        ]
                                    }
                                ]
                            }
                        ]
                    },
                    {
